1	# Membrane MCP Server
2	
3	<a href="https://getmembrane.com/">
4	  <img width="1148" alt="Screenshot 2025-07-07 at 23 03 05" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/39f6cc74-a689-4657-91f3-ee8358c05e31" />
5	</a>
6	
7	The Membrane MCP Server is a [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/introduction) server, it provides actions for connected integrations on Membrane as tools.
8	
9	Here's our official [AI Agent Example](https://github.com/membranehq/ai-agent-example) that shows you how to use this MCP server in your application.
10	
11	### 📋 Prerequisites
12	
13	- Node.js (v18 or higher)
14	- A [Membrane](https://getmembrane.com) account
15	
16	### ⚙️ Installation
17	
18	```bash
19	git clone https://github.com/membranehq/mcp-server.git
20	cd mcp-server
21	npm install
22	npm run build
23	```
24	
25	### 🛠️ Local Development
26	
27	To run the development server locally, start it with:
28	
29	```bash
30	npm run dev
31	```
32	
33	The server will be live at `http://localhost:3000` ⚡️
34	
35	### 🧪 Running tests
36	
37	```bash
38	# Run the server in test mode
39	npm run start:test
40	
41	# then run tests
42	npm test
43	```
44	
45	### 🚀 Deployment
46	
47	Deploy your own instance of this MCP server to any cloud hosting service of your choice.
48	
49	#### 🐳 Docker
50	
51	The project includes a Dockerfile for easy containerized deployment.
52	
53	```bash
54	docker build -t membrane-mcp-server .
55	docker run -p 3000:3000 membrane-mcp-server
56	```
57	
58	### 🔗 Connecting to the MCP server
59	
60	This MCP server support two transports:
61	
62	| Transport                                                                                                              | Endpoint | Status                                                                 |
63	| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
64	| [SSE](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/transports#server-sent-events-sse-deprecated) (Server‑Sent Events) | `/sse`   | 🔴 **Deprecated** — deprecated as of November 5, 2024 in MCP spec      |
65	| [HTTP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/transports#streamable-http) (Streamable HTTP)                     | `/mcp`   | 🟢 **Recommended** — replaces SSE and supports bidirectional streaming |
66	
67	### 🔐 Authentication
68	
69	Provide a [Membrane access token](https://docs.getmembrane.com/docs/authentication#access-token) via query or `Authorization` header:
70	
71	```http
72	?token=ACCESS_TOKEN
73	Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN
74	```
75	
76	**SSE** (Deprecated)
77	
78	```js
79	await client.connect(
80	  new SSEClientTransport(
81	    new URL(
82	      `https://<HOSTED_MCP_SERVER_URL>/sse`
83	    )
84	    {
85	      requestInit: {
86	        headers: {
87	          Authorization: `Bearer ${ACCESS_TOKEN}`,
88	        },
89	      },
90	    }
91	  )
92	);
93	```
94	
95	**Streamable HTTP** (Recommended)
96	
97	```js
98	
99	await client.connect(
100	  new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(
101	    new URL(`https://<HOSTED_MCP_SERVER_URL>/mcp`)
102	    {
103	      requestInit: {
104	        headers: {
105	          Authorization: `Bearer ${ACCESS_TOKEN}`,
106	        },
107	      },
108	    }
109	  )
110	);
111	```
112	
113	### ⚡ Static vs Dynamic Mode
114	
115	By default, the MCP server runs in **static mode**, which means it returns **all available tools** (actions) for all connected integrations.
116	
117	With **dynamic mode** (`?mode=dynamic`), the server will only return **one tool**: `enable-tools`. You can use this tool to selectively enable the tools you actually need for that session.
118	
119	In dynamic mode, your implementation should figure out which tools are most relevant to the user's query. Once you've identified them, prompt the LLM to call the `enable-tools` tool with the appropriate list.
120	
121	Want to see how this works in practice? Check out our [AI Agent Example](https://github.com/membranehq/ai-agent-example).
122	
123	```ts
124	import { Client } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js';
125	import { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/streamableHttp.js';
126	
127	const client = new Client({
128	  name: 'example-membrane-mcp-client',
129	  version: '1.0.0',
130	});
131	
132	const transport = new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(
133	  new URL(`https://<HOSTED_MCP_SERVER_URL>/mcp?mode=dynamic`),
134	  {
135	    requestInit: {
136	      headers: {
137	        Authorization: `Bearer ${ACCESS_TOKEN}`,
138	      },
139	    },
140	  }
141	);
142	
143	await client.connect(transport);
144	
145	await client.callTool({
146	  name: 'enable-tools',
147	  arguments: {
148	    tools: ['gmail-send-email', 'gmail-read-email'],
149	  },
150	});
151	```
152	
153	### 🔧 Getting tools for a specific integrations
154	
155	In static mode, the MCP server fetches tools from all active connections associated with the provided token.
156	
157	You can choose to only fetch tools for a specific integration by passing the `apps` query parameter: `/mcp?apps=google-calendar,google-docs`
158	
159	### 💬 Chat Session Management (Experimental)
160	
161	The MCP server (streamable-http transport only) supports persistent chat sessions. Include an `x-chat-id` header in your requests to automatically track sessions for that specific chat. This is an experimental feature that we provide in addition to standard MCP sessions.
162	
163	**Starting a new chat session:**
164	
165	```http
166	POST /mcp
167	Authorization: Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN
168	x-chat-id: my-awesome-chat-123
169	```
170	
171	**Retrieving your chat sessions:**
172	
173	```http
174	GET /mcp/sessions
175	Authorization: Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN
176	```
177	
178	**Response:**
179	
180	```json
181	{
182	  "my-awesome-chat-123": "session-uuid-1",
183	  "another-chat-456": "session-uuid-2"
184	}
185	```
186	
187	This feature lets you use same session for a conversation. Check out our [AI Agent Example](https://github.com/membranehq/ai-agent-example) to see how this works in practice.
188	
189	### Configuring other MCP clients
190	
191	#### 📝 Cursor
192	
193	To use this server with Cursor, update the `~/.cursor/mcp.json` file:
194	
195	```json
196	{
197	  "mcpServers": {
198	    "membrane": {
199	      "url": "https://<HOSTED_MCP_SERVER_URL>/sse?token={ACCESS_TOKEN}"
200	    }
201	  }
202	}
203	```
204	
205	Restart Cursor for the changes to take effect.
206	
207	#### 🤖 Claude Desktop
208	
209	To use this server with Claude, update the config file (Settings > Developer > Edit Config):
210	
211	```json
212	{
213	  "mcpServers": {
214	    "membrane": {
215	      "url": "https://<HOSTED_MCP_SERVER_URL>/sse?token={ACCESS_TOKEN}"
216	    }
217	  }
218	}
219	```
220	
221	### 🔧 Troubleshooting
222	
223	- Ensure your access token is valid and you're generating it according to [these instructions](https://docs.getmembrane.com/docs/authentication#access-token)
224	- Check the MCP server logs for any errors or issues during startup or connection attempts.
225	- Use the [MCP Inspector](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@modelcontextprotocol/inspector) for testing and debugging
226	

